window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["advanced-rag"]=[{"id":"advanced-rag-f0001","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"Why is a vector database better described as a compute engine for vector data than as a database?","a":"<p>Its main job is not storing rows but running similarity computations over long lists of numbers (embeddings with hundreds to thousands of dimensions). The \"database\" part (persistence, filtering) supports that search. It is built to answer \"which vectors are closest to this one\" quickly, often with approximate nearest-neighbor indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0002","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"Why are embeddings taken from the second-to-last layer of a deep neural network?","a":"<p>The final layer applies the task-specific prediction (a class label, for example). The layer just before it holds what the network learned about the input, so it is a general-purpose representation of meaning. That vector is reused for similarity search instead of the prediction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0003","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"In a RAG system, why must the query be embedded with the same model that embedded the stored data?","a":"<p>Vectors can only be compared if they have the same dimensions and live in the same embedding space. A different model gives vectors of a different size, or the same size with unrelated meaning for each coordinate. Distances between them are meaningless. If you change the embedding model, you must re-embed the whole corpus.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0004","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What does this code get wrong, and why does it fail?","a":"<p>A text-only model cannot embed an image. The data type must match the model type: text models for text, image models for images. A multimodal model such as CLIP can handle both, but you still choose the right encoder for each input type.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\ntext_model = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&quot;)\nimage_embedding = text_model.encode(image)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0005","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What does <code>SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\").encode(text)</code> return, and what is the size of that vector?","a":"<p>It returns a dense NumPy vector for the text, with 384 dimensions for this model. Larger models such as <code>all-mpnet-base-v2</code> return 768 dimensions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0006","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"Why does a plain LLM need RAG to answer questions about your private or recent data?","a":"<p>An LLM predicts the next token from patterns learned on public training data up to a cutoff date. It has never seen your documents and cannot look them up. RAG retrieves relevant passages at query time and puts them in the prompt, so the model answers from that context instead of guessing. This also reduces hallucination, and the data can be updated without retraining.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0007","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"List the steps of a RAG query pipeline in order and say what data flows between them.","a":"<ol>\n  <li>The question is embedded by the embedding model. 2) The vector database runs a similarity search with that vector. 3) The top-K matching chunks come back as context. 4) The LLM receives the question plus that context in its prompt. 5) The LLM writes the answer. The context is plain text, not vectors, because an LLM cannot read embeddings.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0008","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"In this RAG sketch, which step is wrong or missing if the LLM never sees the retrieved text?","a":"<p><code>context</code> is built but never placed into the prompt, so the model answers from its training data only. The user message needs to include the context, for example <code>f\"Context:\\n{context}\\n\\nQuestion: {query}\"</code>, and a system message such as \"Answer based on the provided context\" keeps the answer grounded.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = vector_db.search(collection=&quot;documents&quot;, query_vector=query_embedding, limit=5)\ncontext = &quot;\\n&quot;.join([r[&quot;text&quot;] for r in results])\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=&quot;gpt-4&quot;,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: f&quot;Question: {query}&quot;}],\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0009","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"In the RAG pipeline, what does <code>limit=5</code> (top-K) trade off?","a":"<p>A larger K raises the chance that the right passage is retrieved (recall), but adds noise, cost and latency, and may overflow the LLM context window. A smaller K is cheaper and more focused but can miss the needed chunk. Choose K together with chunk size so that several chunks fit comfortably in the context window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0010","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"Why do RAG apps usually pass the retrieved text to the LLM instead of the retrieved vectors?","a":"<p>An embedding is a one-way, lossy representation. An LLM cannot decode it back to text. The vector database finds matches by similarity, so the original text must be stored alongside the vector (usually in metadata) and returned with the hit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0011","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What are the three components of RAG data preprocessing, and what does each do?","a":"<p>Chunking splits large text into smaller blocks. Embeddings turn each block into a vector that captures its meaning. Metadata is data stored alongside each vector (original text, section, author, date) that supports filtering and customization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0012","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"Why do we chunk documents instead of embedding a whole document, given that LLM context windows are large?","a":"<p>The context window is a cap, not a target amount. One vector for a long document blurs many topics together, so retrieval gets less precise. Small coherent chunks give focused vectors, bring back only relevant text, cost fewer tokens, and let several different passages fit in the prompt. Embedding models also have their own input limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0013","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What is the difference between dense and sparse embeddings, and how is each created?","a":"<p>Dense embeddings have few zero values and are produced by machine-learning models. Sparse embeddings have mostly zeros (over half) and come from algorithms such as TF-IDF and BM25. RAG mostly uses dense vectors for semantic matching. Sparse vectors are good at exact keyword matching, and the two are often combined in hybrid search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0014","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What is the main use of metadata in a vector search, and why is it applied before the similarity comparison?","a":"<p>Filtering. Metadata (date, author, section, document type) narrows the candidate set so the similarity search only runs on relevant records. This gives more accurate results and often faster queries than filtering the results afterward, which could leave fewer than K good hits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0015","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"In a vector store record that holds <code>id</code>, <code>text</code>, <code>embedding</code> and <code>metadata</code>, why would you keep <code>chunk_index</code> and <code>page</code> in the metadata?","a":"<p>They let you cite where an answer came from, fetch neighboring chunks for more context, restore the original order, and filter to a page or section. They cost little to store and are hard to rebuild later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0016","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"What are the three C's of good chunks, and what does each require?","a":"<p>Consumable: the chunk fits the embedding model's input limit, and several chunks (depending on top-K) fit in the LLM's context window. Coherent: it does not start or end in the middle of a word, clause or sentence, so it makes sense alone. Contextual: it holds the information needed to answer a question about it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0017","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"A chunk reads \"killed the cat\". Which of the three C's does it break, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>It breaks coherence, because it starts mid-clause. Split on sentence or paragraph boundaries using separators so chunks begin and end at natural breaks. If the text is coherent but lacks needed context (a sentence whose meaning depends on the previous one), add overlap or larger chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0018","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"A chunk is coherent but retrieval answers are often incomplete. Which of the three C's is likely missing, and what can you change?","a":"<p>The chunk is not contextual enough. Increase chunk size, add overlap so context carries over from the previous chunk, store parent paragraphs or section headers in metadata, or use a small-to-big retrieval scheme.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0019","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"Why does the \"consumable\" rule say at least three chunks should fit in the LLM's context window?","a":"<p>Retrieval returns top-K chunks, and the prompt also holds the question, instructions and the answer space. If a single chunk fills the window you could not include several results. Size chunks so that K chunks plus the rest of the prompt stay within the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0020","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What are the three chunking parameters, and what does each control?","a":"<p>Chunk size is how many characters (or tokens) each chunk holds. Chunk overlap is how much text is repeated between consecutive chunks. Separators are the characters the splitter prefers to cut on, such as paragraph breaks, newlines and periods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0021","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What is a reasonable starting chunk size for prose, and what decides whether to change it?","a":"<p>Start around one paragraph, roughly 100 words or 500 characters. Then tune it by the structure of the data and by measuring retrieval quality. Short Q&amp;A or chat data needs smaller units, and dense technical documents may need larger ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0022","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"Why use chunk overlap, and what is the cost of too much overlap?","a":"<p>Overlap repeats the end of the previous chunk (often its last sentence) at the start of the next, so context is not lost at the cut. Too much overlap inflates the number of chunks, storage and embedding cost, and returns near-duplicate results that waste top-K slots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0023","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"In LangChain's <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code>, how does the <code>separators</code> list work?","a":"<p>It is a priority order. The splitter first tries to split on paragraphs (<code>\"\\n\\n\"</code>). Any piece still larger than <code>chunk_size</code> is split with the next separator, then the next, down to single characters (<code>\"\"</code>) as a last resort. This keeps large units, such as paragraphs and sentences, intact when possible.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=200,\n    chunk_overlap=50,\n    separators=[&quot;\\n\\n&quot;, &quot;\\n&quot;, &quot;. &quot;, &quot; &quot;, &quot;&quot;],\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0024","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"When is it better to let chunks run slightly over <code>chunk_size</code> rather than cut exactly at the limit?","a":"<p>When a hard cut would break a sentence. For example, with a size of 500 and a 520-character paragraph, keeping the whole paragraph gives a more coherent chunk. The cost is slightly larger chunks, which is fine as long as they still fit the embedding model's input limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0025","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-3-1-document-data-papers-reports-docs-bl","sec":"advanced-rag-s006","q":"Why is chunking regular document data (reports, blogs, API docs) considered the easy case?","a":"<p>Blocks are fairly regular in size and structure, with little variation. A fixed chunk size with modest overlap and a split on blank lines (paragraphs) usually gives coherent chunks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0026","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-3-1-document-data-papers-reports-docs-bl","sec":"advanced-rag-s006","q":"How would you configure LangChain's <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> for a research paper in plain text?","a":"<p>Use a chunk size of a few hundred characters, a small overlap (around 10%), and separators ordered from paragraph to sentence to word, for example <code>[\"\\n\\n\", \"\\n\", \". \", \" \"]</code>. Add section titles and page numbers as metadata so retrieved chunks can be cited and filtered.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0027","card":"q-advanced-rag-007-3-2-q-a-transcripts-podcasts-lectures-am","sec":"advanced-rag-s007","q":"Why is it a problem to chunk Q&amp;A transcripts like ordinary prose?","a":"<p>Questions are short and answers are long, and an answer often means nothing without its question. Fixed-size chunking can separate them or cut an answer off from the question that gives it meaning, which hurts retrieval and the LLM's answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0028","card":"q-advanced-rag-007-3-2-q-a-transcripts-podcasts-lectures-am","sec":"advanced-rag-s007","q":"How should you structure chunks and metadata for a Q&amp;A transcript?","a":"<p>Keep questions and answers as separate chunks, and link them with metadata such as <code>role</code> (question or answer) and a shared <code>pair_id</code>. Their sizes differ widely, so separate chunks give each a focused embedding, and a user query often resembles a question or an answer on its own. After retrieving an answer you can fetch its question (or the reverse) by <code>pair_id</code> and give the LLM both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0029","card":"q-advanced-rag-008-3-3-chat-transcripts-support-chats-texts","sec":"advanced-rag-s008","q":"What makes chat transcripts harder to chunk than documents?","a":"<p>Message sizes vary from one word to a paragraph, and the links between messages are unpredictable, since one person may send several messages in a row and replies may refer to earlier turns. Fixed-size splitting would mix speakers and cut messages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0030","card":"q-advanced-rag-008-3-3-chat-transcripts-support-chats-texts","sec":"advanced-rag-s008","q":"What metadata would you store for each chat message chunk, and why?","a":"<p>Sender, timestamp, message index and a conversation ID. They let you separate speakers, restore order, rebuild the conversation around a hit, and filter by customer, agent or time. Separators that mark who is speaking help the splitter cut at turn boundaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0031","card":"q-advanced-rag-008-3-3-chat-transcripts-support-chats-texts","sec":"advanced-rag-s008","q":"A support-chat message such as \"Order #12345\" is too short to embed well. What can you do?","a":"<p>Group consecutive messages from the same sender into one chunk, or retrieve the surrounding messages using the conversation ID and message index, so the retrieved text carries enough context. Keeping the sender in metadata avoids mixing the customer's words with the agent's.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0032","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"What three decisions do you make when using embeddings for retrieval?","a":"<p>Which embedding model to use, what exactly to embed (the chunk itself, or a smaller or larger unit), and which distance metric to compare vectors with.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0033","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"How do you choose an embedding model for a data type?","a":"<p>Match the type: image models such as ResNet50 for images, text models such as Sentence Transformers for text, audio models such as Whisper for audio. For text, compare candidates on the Hugging Face MTEB leaderboard for your task and language, then test on your own data. Specialized data such as CSVs may need its own model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0034","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"Are embedding models and LLMs the same thing?","a":"<p>No. Embedding models are neural networks built to output vectors for comparison. LLMs can also produce embeddings, but their purpose is generating text, and a dedicated embedding model is usually smaller, cheaper and better for retrieval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0035","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"How would you embed non-English text for a RAG system, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Use a model trained on that language. A multilingual LLM-based embedder is easy to adopt but needs more compute. A language-specific model (search MTEB for French, Polish, Chinese and others) is usually more efficient and gives better results for that language. A model trained on one language will do poorly on another.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0036","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"Which factors matter when picking an embedding model?","a":"<p>Embedding size (dimensions, such as 384, 768 or 1536), model size, and training data (language, text style such as chat versus essays, and amount). Bigger vectors and bigger models capture finer detail but cost more to compute, store and compare.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0037","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"What happens if you compare a 384-dimension embedding with a 768-dimension embedding?","a":"<p>It fails with a shape mismatch, because distance metrics need vectors of equal length. Even when two models happen to give equal sizes, their spaces are unrelated, so the result would be meaningless. Use one model for both indexing and querying.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>small_emb = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&quot;).encode(&quot;Hello world&quot;)\nlarge_emb = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-mpnet-base-v2&quot;).encode(&quot;Hello world&quot;)\n# cosine_similarity(small_emb, large_emb)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0038","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"What is the trade-off between a small model like <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> and a larger one like <code>all-mpnet-base-v2</code>?","a":"<p>The small model (384 dimensions) is faster and cheaper, with less storage per vector. The larger model (768 dimensions) captures finer distinctions and usually retrieves better, but it needs more compute and memory. Pick based on the quality you measure against your latency and cost budget.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0039","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"Why should the training data of an embedding model affect your choice?","a":"<p>A model only represents well what it saw in training. A model trained on Chinese will do poorly on Arabic, and one trained on formal essays may do poorly on informal chat. Match the model's language and text style to your corpus.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0040","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"Name two algorithmic (non-neural) methods that produce sparse vectors, and say what they are good at.","a":"<p>TF-IDF and BM25 (a bag-of-words family that scores term frequency and rarity). They score lexical overlap, so they are strong at exact keywords, names and codes, where dense embeddings may be weak. Learned sparse models such as SPLADE are neural and also expand terms, but they are not in this group. Dense vectors are stronger at meaning, so hybrid search combines both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0041","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What is the small-to-big (sentence-to-paragraph) embedding strategy, and why use it?","a":"<p>You embed individual sentences but store the whole paragraph in metadata, and return the paragraph when a sentence matches. Short sentences give precise vectors for matching, and the paragraph provides context and coherence for the LLM. Use it when single sentences are too small to answer from.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0042","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What is the big-to-small embedding strategy, and when does it help?","a":"<p>You embed the whole paragraph and store the individual sentences in metadata. The match is on the paragraph's meaning, and the sentences can be post-processed before the LLM sees them. It helps when sentences alone do not make sense or are hard to split, for example a period after \"Mr.\" breaks naive sentence splitting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0043","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"Compare small-to-big and big-to-small: which unit is embedded, which is returned, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Small-to-big embeds sentences and returns paragraphs. Big-to-small embeds paragraphs and returns sentences. Choose small-to-big when you want precise matching and richer context in the prompt. Choose big-to-small when the paragraph is the meaningful unit for matching but you want to hand the LLM only selected sentences.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0044","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"Why is embedding each chunk as-is not enough for production RAG?","a":"<p>It works for basic tasks, but the unit that matches best is often not the unit that answers best. Strategies like small-to-big decouple the embedded text from the returned text, improving retrieval even when the chunks are already well formed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0045","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What is wrong with splitting sentences using <code>paragraph.split(\". \")</code> before embedding?","a":"<p>It splits on any period followed by a space, so \"Mr.\" is cut off as its own sentence. Use a proper sentence tokenizer (such as NLTK or spaCy), or avoid sentence splitting by embedding the paragraph (big-to-small). The last sentence also keeps its final period while the others lose theirs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>paragraph = &quot;Mr. Smith went to Washington. He met with the committee.&quot;\nsentences = paragraph.split(&quot;. &quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0046","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"How do Euclidean distance, cosine similarity and dot product differ for dense embeddings?","a":"<p>Euclidean is the straight-line distance between two points, so it is sensitive to magnitude. Cosine similarity measures the angle between vectors and ignores magnitude. Dot product is the sum of element-wise products and is influenced by both angle and magnitude. Cosine is the common default for text embeddings, and it needs the most computation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0047","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"Why is the inner (dot) product computationally the cheapest of the three dense metrics?","a":"<p>It is cosine similarity without the division by the vector lengths. There are no norms to compute, only multiplications and one sum. If vectors are normalized to unit length, dot product and cosine give the same ranking, so many systems normalize once and use dot product.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0048","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"What is the output of this code for cosine similarity?","a":"<p><code>0.9746</code>. The dot product is 32, the norms are about 3.7417 and 8.775, and 32 divided by their product is about 0.9746. A value near 1 means the vectors point in nearly the same direction.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import numpy as np\na = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\nb = np.array([4.0, 5.0, 6.0])\nprint(round(float(np.dot(a, b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b))), 4))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0049","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"Which metric would you choose if embedding magnitude carries meaning, and which if only direction does?","a":"<p>If only direction matters (typical for text embeddings), use cosine similarity. If magnitude carries meaning, such as popularity or confidence encoded in the vector, Euclidean or dot product keep that information. Always follow the metric the embedding model was trained with.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0050","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-sparse-binary-vecto","sec":"advanced-rag-s013","q":"What do Hamming distance and Jaccard distance measure for binary vectors?","a":"<p>Hamming counts the positions where two binary vectors differ (XOR, then count the ones). Jaccard distance is 1 minus Jaccard similarity, where similarity is the size of the intersection divided by the size of the union of the set bits. Jaccard ignores positions where both are 0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0051","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-sparse-binary-vecto","sec":"advanced-rag-s013","q":"What does this code print for the Hamming and Jaccard distance?","a":"<p>It prints <code>3</code> and <code>0.6</code>. Three positions differ, so Hamming is 3. The intersection has 2 set bits (positions 0 and 3), the union has 5, so similarity is 0.4 and distance is 0.6.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import numpy as np\na = np.array([1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1])\nb = np.array([1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0])\nprint(np.sum(a ^ b))\nprint(round(1 - np.sum(a &amp; b) / np.sum(a | b), 4))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0052","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-sparse-binary-vecto","sec":"advanced-rag-s013","q":"When would you choose Jaccard over Hamming for sparse binary vectors?","a":"<p>When most bits are 0, as in sparse keyword or tag vectors. Shared zeros say nothing about similarity, and Hamming counts only differences, so Jaccard's focus on shared set bits relative to all set bits is more informative. Hamming is cheaper and fine for fixed-length binary codes, such as quantized or hashed embeddings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0053","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"What are the two kinds of metadata in a RAG pipeline, and what is each used for?","a":"<p>Chunking metadata is produced during chunking (sentence number, subtitle, section header) and gives context plus filtering to one section. Non-chunking metadata comes from outside chunking (author, last-updated date, document title) and is mainly used for filtering, for example \"written by me\" or \"updated last month\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0054","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"Should metadata live in the vector store or in an external relational database, and why?","a":"<p>You can do either. Storing it directly in the vector store is the more common choice for RAG, because filtering and similarity search happen in one query and are faster and simpler. An external relational database suits cases where metadata is large, changes often, or is joined with other tables, at the cost of an extra lookup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0055","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"What does it mean that without metadata a vector database only compares numbers?","a":"<p>A vector alone has no meaning to the application. Metadata links it back to the original text, document, author, date and position, and enables filtered search and citations. That is what turns a similarity index into a usable retrieval system.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0056","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"A user wants answers only from documents updated in the last month. How do you support this?","a":"<p>Store a last-updated date as metadata on every chunk at ingestion, and apply a metadata filter (a date range) in the vector store query, so only matching chunks are searched for similarity. This needs the field to be set at ingestion time and filterable in the store you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0057","card":"q-advanced-rag-015-chunk","sec":"advanced-rag-s015","q":"In LangChain, what does <code>CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=128, chunk_overlap=32)</code> do, and in what units?","a":"<p>It splits text into chunks of about 128 characters, where each chunk repeats the last 32 characters of the previous one. Units are characters by default, not tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0058","card":"q-advanced-rag-015-chunk","sec":"advanced-rag-s015","q":"In LangChain, why wrap each chunk in a <code>Document</code> object before building a vector store?","a":"<p>A <code>Document</code> pairs <code>page_content</code> (the chunk text) with a <code>metadata</code> dict. Vector stores and retrievers in LangChain work with <code>Document</code> objects, so wrapping lets the text and its metadata travel together through the retrieval chain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0059","card":"q-advanced-rag-015-chunk","sec":"advanced-rag-s015","q":"In LangChain, why create the <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code> once and not for every file inside the loop?","a":"<p>The splitter is configuration only and holds no per-file state, so creating it once is cleaner and cheaper. It makes no difference to the output. Reusing one configured splitter also guarantees every file is chunked the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0060","card":"q-advanced-rag-016-add-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s016","q":"Why use <code>enumerate</code> over the chunks when building <code>Document</code> objects with metadata?","a":"<p><code>enumerate</code> gives each chunk its position <code>i</code> in the document. Storing it as <code>chunk_number</code> records where the chunk sits, lets you restore order, fetch neighboring chunks, and cite location. Without it the position is lost after chunks are embedded.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n    doc = Document(\n        page_content=chunk,\n        metadata={&quot;doc_title&quot;: file, &quot;chunk_number&quot;: i},\n    )</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0061","card":"q-advanced-rag-016-add-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s016","q":"What does the LangChain retriever return when <code>Document</code> objects carry metadata, and how does this help the LLM?","a":"<p>Each returned <code>Document</code> has both <code>page_content</code> and <code>metadata</code>. The metadata (such as document title and chunk number) can be printed, used for citations, or included in the prompt, so the LLM can say where information came from.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0062","card":"q-advanced-rag-017-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"What is FAISS, and why is it used in a RAG demo?","a":"<p>FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search) is a library for fast similarity search over dense vectors, and many vector databases are built on it. It runs in-process with no server, so it is convenient for prototypes. For production you usually want a database that adds persistence, filtering, scaling and access control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0063","card":"q-advanced-rag-017-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"In LangChain, what does <code>FAISS.from_documents(documents=all_docs, embedding=embedding_function)</code> do?","a":"<p>It embeds the <code>page_content</code> of every <code>Document</code> with the given embedding object, builds a FAISS index from the vectors, and keeps the documents and their metadata so that retrieval can return them. You pass the embedding object, not precomputed vectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0064","card":"q-advanced-rag-017-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"Where does LangChain's <code>HuggingFaceEmbeddings</code> class come from, and what is its default model?","a":"<p>In LangChain 0.2 and later, import it with <code>from langchain_huggingface import HuggingFaceEmbeddings</code> (package <code>langchain-huggingface</code>). In the 0.1 era it lived in <code>langchain_community.embeddings</code>, and that path is deprecated. The default model is <code>sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2</code>, with 768 dimensions. Import paths change between versions, so check the docs for the version you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0065","card":"q-advanced-rag-017-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"Why must the embedding object passed to <code>FAISS.from_documents</code> also be used for queries?","a":"<p>The vector store keeps the embedding function and uses it to embed queries, so the query vector has the same dimensions and space as the stored ones. If you rebuild or reload the index, pass the same embedding model, or similarity search will fail or return noise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0066","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"In LangChain, what does <code>vector_store.as_retriever()</code> do?","a":"<p>It wraps the vector store in a retriever interface. The retriever takes a query string, embeds it with the store's embedding function, runs the similarity search, and returns a list of <code>Document</code> objects. That hides the embedding and query steps and plugs into chains.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0067","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"How many documents does a default LangChain <code>as_retriever()</code> return, and how do you change it?","a":"<p>Four by default (top-K of 4). Change it with <code>as_retriever(search_kwargs={\"k\": 8})</code>. Higher K improves recall but adds noise and prompt tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0068","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"Why does retrieval in RAG look like the LLM does the querying?","a":"<p>In a chain, the user's question is passed to the retriever automatically and the result is injected into the prompt. The user never writes a vector query. The LLM answers from the retrieved context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0069","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"In LangChain, how do you check what the retriever returns before involving the LLM?","a":"<p>Call <code>retriever.invoke(query)</code> and print each result's <code>page_content</code> and <code>metadata</code>. In LangChain 0.1.x and later <code>invoke</code> is the standard method (older code used <code>get_relevant_documents</code>, which is deprecated). Checking retrieval alone separates retrieval problems from generation problems.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = vector_store.as_retriever()\nresults = retriever.invoke(&quot;What is Binaryville about?&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0070","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"In a LangChain Expression Language chain, what does <code>{\"context\": retriever, \"question\": RunnablePassthrough()}</code> do?","a":"<p>The dict runs both entries on the same input string. <code>retriever</code> turns the question into retrieved documents for <code>context</code>, and <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> forwards the question unchanged for <code>question</code>. The resulting dict fills the prompt template's <code>{context}</code> and <code>{question}</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chain = (\n    {&quot;context&quot;: retriever, &quot;question&quot;: RunnablePassthrough()}\n    | prompt\n    | llm\n    | StrOutputParser()\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0071","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"What is the role of each step in that chain: <code>prompt</code>, <code>llm</code>, <code>StrOutputParser()</code>?","a":"<p><code>prompt</code> (a <code>ChatPromptTemplate</code>) fills the <code>{context}</code> and <code>{question}</code> placeholders and builds the model input. <code>llm</code> produces the response. <code>StrOutputParser()</code> converts the model output object into a plain string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0072","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"In LangChain, why use <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> for the question?","a":"<p>The question has to reach the prompt unchanged while the same input also goes to the retriever. <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> forwards its input as-is, so the final dict has both the retrieved context and the original question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0073","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"What goes wrong if the prompt template has a <code>{context}</code> placeholder but the chain's input dict has no <code>context</code> key?","a":"<p>The template cannot fill the variable and raises an error about a missing input variable. Every placeholder in the prompt must be supplied by a key in the dict that flows into it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0074","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"Why does the retriever's output work as a prompt string for <code>{context}</code> in this chain, even though it returns <code>Document</code> objects?","a":"<p>The template converts the list of <code>Document</code> objects to text when it formats the prompt. That text is the string form (repr) of each <code>Document</code>, so it includes the metadata fields as well as <code>page_content</code>. For a clean layout, add a step that joins only the <code>page_content</code> values into one string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0075","card":"q-advanced-rag-020-challenge-1-cite-document-sources-prompt","sec":"advanced-rag-s020","q":"How can a RAG app make the LLM name the documents its answer came from, without changing the pipeline?","a":"<p>Chunk metadata (such as <code>doc_title</code>) is already returned with each retrieved <code>Document</code>, so the prompt only needs an instruction such as \"Cite the documents you found the answer in\". The metadata must reach the prompt text, and the model should be told to use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0076","card":"q-advanced-rag-020-challenge-1-cite-document-sources-prompt","sec":"advanced-rag-s020","q":"Why is relying on a prompt instruction alone for citations not fully reliable, and how can you make it more reliable?","a":"<p>The model may invent, omit or mis-assign document names. For stronger guarantees, put the title or ID into each context passage explicitly, ask for structured output with a list of document IDs, and verify the cited IDs against the retrieved set in code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0077","card":"q-advanced-rag-021-challenge-2-oversized-chunks-custom-sepa","sec":"advanced-rag-s021","q":"In LangChain, why does <code>CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=128, chunk_overlap=32)</code> produce chunks larger than 128 characters on text that has no blank lines?","a":"<p>Its default <code>separator</code> is <code>\"\\n\\n\"</code>. The splitter only cuts at that separator, so if none occurs within the size window it cannot split and emits a chunk larger than <code>chunk_size</code>, with a warning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0078","card":"q-advanced-rag-021-challenge-2-oversized-chunks-custom-sepa","sec":"advanced-rag-s021","q":"How do you fix oversized chunks from <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code>, and what is the effect?","a":"<p>Set <code>separator=\"\\n\"</code> so it can cut at any line break. Chunks now fit the size limit only if the individual lines are shorter than <code>chunk_size</code>; a single longer line still stays whole. Chunks end at line boundaries. Alternatively use <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code>, which falls back from paragraphs to lines to sentences to words automatically.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=128, chunk_overlap=32, separator=&quot;\\n&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0079","card":"q-advanced-rag-021-challenge-2-oversized-chunks-custom-sepa","sec":"advanced-rag-s021","q":"What does <code>is_separator_regex</code> control in <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code>?","a":"<p>By default (<code>False</code>) the separator is treated as a literal string. Set it to <code>True</code> to treat the separator as a regular expression, which allows patterns such as splitting on a speaker label.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0080","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"What is the difference between semantic and pixel (visual) image embeddings, and which does RAG use?","a":"<p>Semantic embeddings capture what the image means. They come from the second-to-last layer of a trained deep network. Pixel embeddings are a compressed version of the raw pixel input and capture what the image literally looks like. RAG uses semantic embeddings, so a photo of a cat matches other cat images or the text \"a cat\" regardless of colors and layout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0081","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"Why are pixel embeddings usually a poor choice for image retrieval in RAG?","a":"<p>Two images of the same object with different lighting, angle or background have very different pixels, so they land far apart. Different objects with similar colors may land close. Similarity in pixel space does not track meaning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0082","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"In PyTorch, how can you get the semantic embedding from a pretrained ResNet50?","a":"<p>Register a forward hook on the layer before the classifier (<code>avgpool</code> in ResNet50) that stores its output, then run the image through the model. The stored 2048-dimension vector is the embedding. The final fully connected layer, which predicts classes, is ignored. In torchvision 0.13 and later, <code>pretrained=True</code> is deprecated and replaced by a <code>weights=</code> argument (for example <code>weights=models.ResNet50_Weights.DEFAULT</code>).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resnet = models.resnet50(pretrained=True)\nresnet.eval()\nresnet.avgpool.register_forward_hook(hook_fn)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0083","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"Why call <code>resnet.eval()</code> and use <code>torch.no_grad()</code> when extracting embeddings?","a":"<p><code>eval()</code> turns off training behavior (dropout, batch-norm updates) so outputs are deterministic. <code>torch.no_grad()</code> skips gradient tracking, which saves memory and time. Without them embeddings can vary between runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0084","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"Why is an image resized, center-cropped and normalized before it goes into a pretrained model?","a":"<p>The network was trained on 224x224 inputs with specific per-channel mean and standard deviation values (from ImageNet). Using the same preprocessing keeps inputs in the distribution the model expects, and skipping it degrades embedding quality.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>preprocess = transforms.Compose([\n    transforms.Resize(256),\n    transforms.CenterCrop(224),\n    transforms.ToTensor(),\n    transforms.Normalize(mean=[0.485, 0.456, 0.406], std=[0.229, 0.224, 0.225]),\n])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0085","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"How does a convolutional layer extract features, and how does max pooling relate to it?","a":"<p>A convolution slides a small learnable filter (for example 3x3) over the image. At each position it multiplies the window element-wise with the filter and sums the result, extracting local patterns such as edges. Max pooling then compresses the output by keeping the maximum of each small region, which reduces size and adds tolerance to small shifts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0086","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"In PyTorch, what are the output shapes after these layers for an input of shape <code>(batch, 3, 224, 224)</code>?","a":"<p>After the convolution: <code>(batch, 16, 224, 224)</code>, because <code>padding=1</code> with a 3x3 kernel keeps the spatial size and 16 filters give 16 channels. After pooling: <code>(batch, 16, 112, 112)</code>, since a 2x2 window with stride 2 halves height and width.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conv_layer = nn.Conv2d(in_channels=3, out_channels=16, kernel_size=3, padding=1)\npool_layer = nn.MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0087","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"How does a Vision Transformer (ViT) process an image?","a":"<p>It splits the image into fixed-size patches (like words), turns each patch into an embedding, prepends a [CLS] token, adds positional encodings, and passes the sequence through transformer encoder layers with self-attention. A head on top handles the task (classification and so on). The [CLS] output can serve as the image embedding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0088","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What are the [CLS] token and positional encoding for in a ViT?","a":"<p>The [CLS] token is an extra learnable token whose final output summarizes the whole image, so it serves as the image-level embedding. Positional encoding tells the model where each patch sits, because attention alone has no sense of order or location.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0089","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"When might you choose a ViT over a CNN, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>ViTs model long-range relationships across the whole image through attention and scale well with large datasets and pretraining. CNNs have built-in locality and need less data, and are often cheaper at small scale. Modern pretrained ViTs are common for embeddings, but a CNN can be enough for small or constrained setups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0090","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"In Hugging Face Transformers, how do you get an image embedding from <code>ViTModel</code> for <code>google/vit-base-patch16-224</code>?","a":"<p>Take the hidden state at index 0 of the sequence, which is the [CLS] token, for each image in the batch. For this base model it is a 768-dimension vector. Averaging the patch tokens is another common option. Note that in Transformers 4.x, <code>ViTFeatureExtractor</code> was deprecated in favor of <code>ViTImageProcessor</code>, so use the processor class in current code.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = model(**inputs)\nimage_embedding = outputs.last_hidden_state[:, 0, :].squeeze().numpy()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0091","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-get-semantic-vectors-openclip-via-langch","sec":"advanced-rag-s024","q":"In LangChain's <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code>, what does <code>embed_image</code> take as input, and what does it return?","a":"<p>It takes a list of image file paths (URIs), not a single path, and returns a list of embedding vectors, one per image. Passing a single string is a common mistake.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0092","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-get-semantic-vectors-openclip-via-langch","sec":"advanced-rag-s024","q":"Why use OpenCLIP rather than a ResNet or ViT classifier to embed images for retrieval?","a":"<p>CLIP-style models are trained to put images and their text descriptions in the same embedding space. That gives semantic image vectors and also lets you search images with text queries. A plain classifier gives image-only vectors with no text alignment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0093","card":"q-advanced-rag-025-store-image-vectors-in-faiss","sec":"advanced-rag-s025","q":"In a LangChain image search with <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code> and FAISS, how do you build the store, and why not call <code>FAISS.from_documents</code> on documents whose <code>page_content</code> is a base64 string?","a":"<p><code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings.embed_documents</code> and <code>embed_query</code> run the CLIP text encoder, so a base64 string would be embedded as meaningless text. Embed the images with <code>embed_image</code> (a list of file paths), then build the index from the precomputed vectors, for example with <code>FAISS.from_embeddings(text_embeddings=[(content, vector), ...], embedding=OpenCLIPEmbeddings(), metadatas=[...])</code>. Keep the base64 (or the path) as the stored content or metadata so the multimodal LLM can receive the image later. Passing <code>embedding=OpenCLIPEmbeddings()</code> lets text queries use the text encoder in the same space.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0094","card":"q-advanced-rag-025-store-image-vectors-in-faiss","sec":"advanced-rag-s025","q":"What does this helper do, and why is <code>\"rb\"</code> mode used?","a":"<p>It reads the file as raw bytes, base64-encodes them, and decodes that to a UTF-8 string. Binary mode (<code>\"rb\"</code>) is required because images are not text. The string form can be stored in a <code>Document</code>, sent in JSON, or placed in a multimodal LLM request.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def encode_image(file_path: str) -&gt; str:\n    with open(file_path, &quot;rb&quot;) as f:\n        return base64.b64encode(f.read()).decode(&quot;utf-8&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0095","card":"q-advanced-rag-026-compare-images-semantically","sec":"advanced-rag-s026","q":"In LangChain, how do you search for images similar to a given image when the store was built from CLIP image vectors?","a":"<p>Embed the query image with <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings.embed_image([path])</code> and search by vector, for example <code>vector_store.similarity_search_by_vector(vec[0], k=3)</code>. Calling <code>retriever.invoke</code> with an image string would run the text encoder, so it is wrong for image queries. The query and stored images then share the same image encoder, so the nearest vectors are semantically similar images.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0096","card":"q-advanced-rag-026-compare-images-semantically","sec":"advanced-rag-s026","q":"Why should the top result of an image-to-image query on an already indexed image be the query image itself, and what would it mean if it were not?","a":"<p>Embedding the same file with the same encoder gives the same vector as the stored one, so its distance is zero and it ranks first. If it does not, something differs between indexing and querying, for example another embedding model or preprocessing, or an approximate index that missed it. It is a quick sanity check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0097","card":"q-advanced-rag-028-10-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s028","q":"What does multimodal mean in RAG, and why do text plus tables count as multimodal for some people but not others?","a":"<p>Multimodal means more than one data type, such as images with text, audio, or video. Each type typically needs different machine-learning models. Combinations such as PDFs with CSVs, or text with tables, are contested because both are \"sight\" and one could treat them as text. They count only if they need separate processing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0098","card":"q-advanced-rag-028-10-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s028","q":"What are the main challenges of multimodal RAG compared with text RAG?","a":"<p>Each data type needs a suitable embedding model, and vectors from different models cannot be compared unless they share one space. You also need to pass non-text results (images) to an LLM that accepts them, handle larger payloads, and evaluate retrieval across types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0099","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"What are the three ways to do multimodal RAG, and which is the most common?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>A single multimodal embedding model and one vector store (the canonical, most common approach). 2) Several models, one per data type, each with its own vector store (rare, manual routing). 3) Several multimodal models (almost never, highest complexity). Start with method 1 unless it cannot cover your data types.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0100","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"Why does one multimodal model with one vector store keep multimodal RAG simple?","a":"<p>Text and images are embedded into the same space with the same dimensions, so you can query one index with either and compare directly. There is one model to run, one index to maintain, and no routing logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0101","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"In method 2 (one model and one vector store per data type), why can't you combine all vectors in a single store?","a":"<p>Different models give different dimensions, and even equal dimensions from different models belong to unrelated spaces, so distances between them are meaningless. You keep separate stores and route queries to the right store by data type, then merge results in your own code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0102","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"When might you use multiple multimodal models, and what is the cost?","a":"<p>When no single model covers all your data types (video, audio, images, tables), or when you want a second model as a \"second opinion\" to re-rank results. The cost is high complexity: multiple models and stores, routing, and merging scores that are not directly comparable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0103","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"How does re-ranking with a second model help retrieval?","a":"<p>The first-stage retriever returns candidates quickly. A second model, often trained on different data or a cross-encoder, re-scores the candidates and reorders them, which fixes some first-stage mistakes. The cost is added latency and compute, so apply it only to the top candidates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0104","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"What is CLIP, and what does each part of the name mean?","a":"<p>CLIP is Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining. It has a text encoder and an image encoder trained on hundreds of millions of image-text pairs. Contrastive means matching pairs are pulled together and non-matching pairs pushed apart. Language-Image means both modalities map into the same embedding space. Pretraining means it is trained once at large scale and reused.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0105","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"Why can CLIP embed text and images into vectors that are directly comparable?","a":"<p>Training on image-caption pairs with a contrastive loss forces a caption's vector and its image's vector to be close, and mismatched ones far apart. Both encoders output the same dimensionality, so a text query can be matched against image vectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0106","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"In LangChain, how do you embed text and images with the same OpenCLIP model so they go into one vector store?","a":"<p>Use <code>embed_documents</code> for text (a list of strings) and <code>embed_image</code> for images (a list of file paths). Both give vectors of the same length in one shared space, so they can be indexed together and queried with either type.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>clip = OpenCLIPEmbeddings()\ntext_embeddings = clip.embed_documents([&quot;A cat sitting on a couch&quot;])\nimage_embeddings = clip.embed_image([&quot;images/cat1.jpg&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0107","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"How do CLIP, GPT-4o and LLaVA differ as multimodal models?","a":"<p>CLIP is a pair of encoders that outputs embeddings for retrieval and is open source. GPT-4o is a general multimodal LLM that reads text and images and writes text, so it is used for the answer step. LLaVA is an open-source model that connects an image encoder to a language model, so it also answers questions about images. Use CLIP-style models to retrieve and LLMs to answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0108","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"Why are multimodal embedding models usually trained on pairs, and which pair is most common?","a":"<p>To align two modalities, training needs examples that say which items belong together. Image-text pairs (an image and its caption) are the most common, which is why image-text search works best.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0109","card":"q-advanced-rag-032-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How can a text query retrieve images in a CLIP-based RAG system?","a":"<p>The CLIP text encoder embeds the query into the shared space, and the vector store returns the nearest image embeddings. The retrieved images are then passed to a multimodal LLM to describe or answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0110","card":"q-advanced-rag-032-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"Why does the multimodal LangChain chain use <code>RunnableLambda(split_image_text)</code> instead of <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> for the context?","a":"<p>The retrieved documents need processing (separating base64 images from text) before use, and <code>RunnablePassthrough</code> only forwards its input unchanged. <code>RunnableLambda</code> wraps an ordinary Python function as a chain step, so custom logic can run in the pipeline.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chain = (\n    {\n        &quot;context&quot;: retriever | RunnableLambda(split_image_text),\n        &quot;question&quot;: RunnablePassthrough(),\n    }\n    | RunnableLambda(lambda x: format_multimodal_prompt({**x[&quot;context&quot;], &quot;question&quot;: x[&quot;question&quot;]}))\n    | llm\n    | StrOutputParser()\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0111","card":"q-advanced-rag-032-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How do you send an image and a text question to a multimodal chat model through LangChain?","a":"<p>Build a <code>HumanMessage</code> whose <code>content</code> is a list of parts: dicts with <code>{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ...}</code> for text and <code>{\"type\": \"image_url\", \"image_url\": {\"url\": \"data:image/png;base64,&lt;data&gt;\"}}</code> for images. The model then sees both. The message format follows the OpenAI chat format, so check your provider's docs for others.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0112","card":"q-advanced-rag-032-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"Why resize base64 images before sending them to a multimodal LLM API?","a":"<p>Image tokens are billed by size and detail, and large payloads add latency and may hit request limits. Resizing to a small size cuts cost and time, at the cost of some detail the model can see.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0113","card":"q-advanced-rag-032-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"What is wrong with using <code>base64.b64decode(s)</code> in a <code>try</code> block as the only check that a string is an image?","a":"<p>Plain text can often decode as base64 without error, so some text chunks may be misclassified as images, and a decodable string need not be an image. A stricter check, such as validating the decoded bytes with an image library, or storing a content-type tag in metadata, is more reliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0114","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"A text query returns the wrong image in a CLIP-based RAG system. What are the likely causes?","a":"<p>Poor data quality (mislabeled or low-quality images), and the limits of the embedding model and its training data: OpenCLIP may not separate fine-grained descriptions well. The retrieval step, not the LLM, is where the error occurs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0115","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"What fixes would you apply to wrong results caused by an embedding model, and when does each apply?","a":"<p>Fine-tune the embedding model when you have labeled pairs of your own data. Improve data quality by removing bad or mislabeled images and adding diverse examples. Use a reranker, a second model that re-scores and reorders the retrieved results. Try a different or larger embedding model as well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0116","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"How do you debug a multimodal RAG answer that looks wrong?","a":"<p>Bypass the LLM and query the retriever directly, printing the top results (such as image paths). If the retrieved items are already wrong, the problem is the embedding model, data or index. If they are right, look at prompt construction and the LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0117","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"Why does \"garbage in, garbage out\" apply to embeddings?","a":"<p>Vectors reflect the data and model they come from. Mislabeled, noisy or unrepresentative data produces misleading vectors, and the nearest neighbors are then wrong. No downstream step can recover information the embeddings do not hold.</p>","hand":true}];
